            <div xml:id="d398">

                <pb facs="0482" n="384" sameAs="frus:frus1920v01/pg384"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">824.00/153: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Argentina</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Stimson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary
                        of State</hi></head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Paraphrase]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Buenos Aires</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1920-11-24T16:00:00-04:00"><hi rendition="#italic">November 24,
                                1920—4 p.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received 11:30
                    p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>252. I was asked by Señor Torello, Acting Minister for Foreign Affairs, at my
                    first audience with him this morning, to ascertain the views of the Government
                    of the United States on the recognition of the present Government of Bolivia.
                    The Acting Minister said that the Government of Argentina desired to act in this
                    matter only in unison with the United States, but, he states, the country is
                    tranquil and the personnel of the present Government is of the <pb facs="0483"
                        n="385" xml:id="pg385"/> best. France has extended recognition, and the
                    present status is one of considerable inconvenience for Argentina, which has so
                    much business with Bolivia.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Stimson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
